Z||000038||Mach10||10-13-2001||06:40 PM||||They use rubber because that particular material is heat-resistand AND flexible. Rubber is also a good insulator. The air charge is sheilded somewhat from ambiant temperatures in the engine bay. Metal soaks up heat and radiates it. Metal intakes aren't as good because of this The shrinkage is on the same order as metal: Talking several thousandths. Not enough to make an appreciable difference in airflow. IMHO, the losses incurred by "shrinking intake" are negligeable when compared to how much the air charge temp shoots up by having a nice heat conductor piping the air in  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Then again, metal looks SO much cooler  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000039||DaRkLoRD||10-13-2001||08:17 PM||||If anyone's concerned about heating up a metal intake tube, why not get it ceramic coated? If it works for headers, I don't see why not for intake tubes.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
